  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 218 PM EST SUN NOV 1 2026\n\nA complex, amplified weather pattern takes shape for much of the \nlong term period, with a mean longwave trough becoming established\nover the center of the country midweek, gradually transitioning \neast as shortwave disturbances round the base of this trough from \nWednesday through Friday and/or Saturday. \n\nA relatively strong cold front will be pushed east by a pair of \nlead shortwaves, approaching Tuesday and Wednesday and crossing \nthe area Wednesday night through Thursday. The surface front will \nbe weakening as it approaches as the shortwaves will be quite \nprogressive and lifting quickly northeast through the Ohio Valley \ntowards the Lower Great Lakes and northern Mid-Atlantic regions. \nModels are struggling with the handling, timing, and strength of \nthe individual waves past this time with a relatively high- \namplitude short-wavelength wave pattern over the region Friday \nthrough next weekend. Thus, while the NBM generally trends dry for\nthe second half of Friday through Sunday, it would not be \nsurprising to see changes to the PoP forecast during this time \nperiod as models hopefully gain clarity on the pattern as in the \ncoming days. \n\nThe main weather impacts will be the potential for some breezy to \nperhaps windy conditions as the front approaches Tuesday night \nthrough Wednesday as the pressure gradient gets squeezed across the \nSouthern Appalachians, with LREF probabilities approaching a max of \naround 35 percent for 40 mph gusts Wednesday over the Black Mountain \narea, with 5 to 10 percent probabilities toward Wayne County and the \nLake Cumberland areas. Otherwise, the other impact to watch for is \nthe low potential for heavy rain and perhaps some thunder with a \nprecipitation band associated with the arrival of the cold front. \nLow-level flow will be southerly to southeasterly, which will \nmitigate the heavy rain threat downwind of the Cumberland Mountains \nthe farther east one goes, but western and southwestern counties in \nour forecast area may see enough of a threat to warrant a Marginal \nRisk for excessive rainfall despite antecedent very dry conditions \nover the last 2 to 3 weeks. \n\nAttention then turns to the next shortwave which moves quickly east \nacross the center of the country and rounds the base of the longwave \ntrough Thursday. Shortwave ridging looks to briefly cross the area \nlater Friday into Saturday, but latest model runs suggest another \nstrong shortwave digging southeast toward the area later Saturday \ninto Sunday of next week, so increased uncertainty exists at the \ntail end of the long term period. \n\nStrong warm advection ahead of the cold front will allow for \ntemperatures to spike well into the 70s Tuesday, with the potential \nfor a few 80-degree readings immediately downwind of the Cumberland \nMountains across southeastern Kentucky. Lows Tuesday night are \nlikely to be quite mild as warm advection and downslope drying \ncontinue, with readings in the upper 50s to lower 60s, though \neastern valleys may be able to dip into the upper 40s to lower 50s \nif sufficient good radiational cooling can occur during Tuesday \nevening. Wednesday's highs will again reach the 70s for highs, \nthough clouds and the approaching cold front and associated \nprecipitation will keep highs from reaching Tuesday's levels. A \ncooldown commences behind the frontal passage Thursday into Friday, \nthough the mean longwave trough remaining to the west will keep the \nupper flow west to southwesterly until upstream ridging moves over \nthe area later Friday into early Saturday. This will then allow for \nlows to drop back into the 30s and 40s for Friday night and Saturday \nnight.",
